            <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#1A1A1A]" style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;">
              How Can You Share Your Countdown With Family and Friends?
            </h2>
          </div>
          <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
            The tool includes a shareable social card, so you can post your countdown or drop it into a group chat. A few ways couples tend to use one:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm sm:text-base list-disc list-inside">
            <li>Post it on round-number days, like 100, 50 and 30 to go.</li>
            <li>Pin it in the wedding party chat so everyone sees the same date.</li>
            <li>Send it to relatives who are booking travel and need a firm sense of time.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
            Check what the card shows before you post it publicly. If your date or venue is private, keep it in group chats.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section: How Can a Saturday Planning Date Help? -->
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="space-y-2">
            <span class="text-xs font-bold text-[#B76E79] uppercase tracking-wider">Weekly Habits</span>
            <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-[#1A1A1A]" style="font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;">
              How Can a Saturday Planning Date Help?
            </h2>
          </div>
          <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
            The Saturday counter suggests a simple habit: treat each Saturday as a short planning session. Thirty to forty-five minutes is plenty. Try this agenda, and log the three tasks in the <a href="/tools/weekly-wedding-planner" class="text-[#B76E79] font-bold underline hover:text-[#a25d66]">Weekly Wedding Planner</a>:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-700 text-sm sm:text-base list-disc list-inside">
            <li>Glance at the countdown and your next milestone.</li>
            <li>Pick three tasks for the week.</li>
            <li>Make one decision you've been putting off.</li>
            <li>Stop, and go do something that has nothing to do with the wedding.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-slate-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
            Over 27 Saturdays, that's about 80 tasks and 27 decisions, without a single all-nighter.
          </p>
        </div>

